-> A transaction is a group of database operations treated as one logical unit of work
=> A transaction is a sequence/group of one or more DB operations executed as a single logical unit, 
where changes can be committed if every operation successful or rolled back if the operation fails

ALL operations succeed             Something fails          Checkpoint(Savepoint) ROLLBACK
        |                                 |                        | 
      COMMIT;                           ROLLBACK;              ROLLBACK to savepoint;

-- MySQL Syntax
START TRANSACTION;
-- SQL statements
COMMIT;
If something goes wrong:
START TRANSACTION;
-- SQL statements
ROLLBACK;    

-- Hands-On Example cheyandii
Create an account table:
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    account_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_name VARCHAR(100),
    balance DECIMAL(10,2)
);

Insert:
INSERT INTO accounts VALUES
(101, 'Bhavani', 10000),
(102, 'Priya', 5000);

Suppose Bhavani transfers ₹2,000 to Priya
We need two operations:
Bhavani  -> -₹2000
Priya  -> +₹2000

Without transaction:
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 2000
WHERE account_id = 101;

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance + 2000
WHERE account_id = 102;

Imagine: Statement 1 ->SUCCESS -  Database/server problem
Statement 2 -> NEVER EXECUTED  - Bhavani lost ₹2,000 - Priya didn't receive ₹2,000 - Your database is inconsistent

-- Then Now Add a Transaction
START TRANSACTION;
                                                  START TRANSACTION
UPDATE accounts                                          |
SET balance = balance - 2000                        Deduct ₹2000
WHERE account_id = 101;                                  |
                                                    Add ₹2000
UPDATE accounts                                          |
SET balance = balance + 2000                  Everything successful?
WHERE account_id = 102;                                  |
                                                      COMMIT
COMMIT;                                -- Now both changes are treated as part of one transaction


-> COMMIT; -- Make the transaction's changes permanent (Confirm / Save)
