ACID Properties
A -> Atomicity (All or nothing)
C -> Consistency ( Valid state -> Valid state)
-> A transaction should take the database from one valid state to another valid state while respecting defined rules and constraints
I -> Isolation
-> Controlling how concurrent transactions can observe and interfere with one another
D -> Durability
-> The committed result survives failures/recovery

╰┈➤ SAVEPOINT
Suppose:
Operation 1
Operation 2
Operation 3
Operation 4

Something goes wrong at operation 4 - Sometimes you don't want to return all the way to the beginning

Example:
START TRANSACTION;                                       START
                                                           |
UPDATE accounts                              SAVEPOINT <- checkpoint
SET balance = balance - 1000                               |
WHERE account_id = 101;                               Operatiom
                                                           |
SAVEPOINT payment_done;                           Something Unwanted
                                                           |
INSERT INTO transaction_log                      ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT
VALUES (...);

ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT payment_done;
COMMIT;

-- Overallga chudandii                              
START TRANSACTION     | Start processing the order                      
SAVEPOINT             | Create a checkpoint                             
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT | Undo the later part and retry                   
RELEASE SAVEPOINT     | Remove the checkpoint                           
COMMIT                | Order successfully completed → permanently save 
ROLLBACK              | Order failed → cancel all transaction changes   

 Concept                                 Purpose                                       
Transaction      | Keep a logical unit of work safe/consistent   
Trigger          | Automatically react to table events           
Stored Procedure | Reusable database operation called explicitly 
View             | Stored query/virtual-table abstraction        


